THE ARENA OF THE STORY  
 
Once ể the designing principle and a one -line description ¦ world, you must find a 
single arena  that marks the physical boundaries of that world. The arena  © basic space of drama.  It is 
ô, unified place  surrounded by some kind of wall. Everything inside the  arena is part ¦. 
Everything outside the arena is  not.  
Many writers, especially novelists and screenwriters,  mistakenly believe  that since ō go 
anywhere,  you should. Ð a  serious mistake, be -cause if you break the single arena of  ì, the 
drama will literally dissipate. Having too  many arenas results in fragmented, inorganic stories.  
The single arena is easiest to maintain ¢ater  because yo u have the natural advantage ¡ 
stage  frame, edged ń curtain. Movies and novels exp¥  arena, but that just makes a unified 
place even more  essential for building the drama.  
 
Creating the Arena  
I'm not suggesting that you adhere ª rigid  "Aristotelian unity of place" that says all action should 
occur  in ô location. There are four major ways of creating  the single arena without destroying the 
variety of place and  action necessary for a good story.  
 
1. Create a large umbrella ¥n  crosscut and  condense.  
In this approach, you describe the largest scope ¡  story somewhere near ž. In effect, you 
start with  the big world ¥ wall that divides it from everything  else. Then you focus ¯ smaller 
worlds with¢ a rena as £ progresses.  
This large umbrella could be as big Ü flat plain of  the West, a city, outer space, or the ocean, or 
it could be as  small as a small town, a house, or a bar.  
P a g e  | 76 This ¼ can be found in Casablanca, Alien,  Spider -Man, L.A. Confidential, The Matrix, Death 
of a Salesman, A Streetcar Named å, Mary Pop -pins,  Groundhog Day, Sunset Boulevard, Nashville, 
Blood  Simple, Meet Me in St. Louis, The Great Gatsby, Shane,  Star Wars, and It's a Wonderful Life.  
 
2. Send § on  a journey through generally  Î area, but one that develops along a 
single  line.  
This approach appears to destroy the single arena, and  when not done properly, it does. One reason 
many journey  stories feel fragmented ā § travels to ế  of very different, unconnected 
places, and each place feels  like a separate episode.  
You can create the ỉ ô arena if the area  â travels through remains 
fundamentally the  same, like a desert, an ocean, a river, or a jungle. But even  here, try ū the 
journey ô recognizable line and  show a simple development ¡ area from ë to  end. 
This gives the area the appearance of unity.  
We see the single -line journey in Titanic, The Wild  Bunch, The Blues Brothers, Jacques Tati's Traffic, 
and The African Queen.  
 
3. Send § on a circular journey through  generally Î area.  
This approach works in much Î way Ü  second one, except that § returns home ­ 
end. You don't get the benefit ¡ single line to give the  ¤ a ỉ a unified, directed path. 
But by going  from home to home, ending back at ž, you  highlight the change in â 
in contrast ª  world, which has remained Î.  
The circular journey is  the foundation for The Wizard of  Oz, Ulysses, Finding Nemo, King Kong, Don 
Quixote,  Big, Heart of Darkness, Beau Geste, Swept Away,  Deliverance, Adventures of Huckleberry Finn, 
Field of  Dreams, and Alice in Wonderland.  
 
4. Make § a fish ť water.  
Start § in one arena. Spend enough time there to  show whatever talents ý that are unique to 
that world.  Then jump â to a second world —without  traveling —and show how the talents the 
hero used ¢  first world, while seeming to be ť place, work equally  well ¢ second.  
This approach is found in Beverly Hills Cop, Crocodile  Dundee, Black Rain, and to a lesser but still 
important  extent in Witness and Dances with Wolves.  
Strictly speaking, fish -out-of-water stories take place in  two distinct arenas, not one. Consequently, 
they often feel  like two -part stories. What holds them together is ¶  hero uses Î talents in 
both places, so ®  comes to feel that while both arenas are superficially quite  differe nt, ầ 
in a deeper sense Î.  
One ¡ keys to using the fish -out-of-water ¼  is to avoid staying too long in ş arena. 
The first arena  © jumping -off point for ð story, which takes place  ¢ second arena. The first  
arena has fulfilled its function  as soon as you show ² talents in that world.  
 
