Scene Weave  
 
WHY ARE JANE AUSTEN and Charles  Dickens such great °s, still delighting ¤s  
even in this high -tech, high -speed world? For one thing,  ầ two ¡ greatest scene weavers of all 
time.  
A scene is generally one action in one time and place. It  © basic unit of what actually happens in 
´ right  now, as ® experiences it. The scene weave is  the sequence ¡se units. To 
be ạ °, you  must create a weave đ like a fine tapestry, picking up  one thread for a 
moment before letting that thread dip back  under the surface be fore appearing again a bit later on.  
The scene weave, also known as ś list, scene  outline, or scene breakdown, © final step 
before writing  your full story or script. It is a list of every scene you believe  will be ¢ final story, along 
¾ tag for any scene in  which a structure step occurs.  
The scene weave is an extremely valuable step ¢  writing process. Like the seven steps, 
character web, and  ¨s sequence, it is a way of seeing how £  fits together beneath the 
surfa ce. 
The scene weave is really an extension of ł. It is  your plot in minute detail. The point ¡ 
scene weave is to  get one last ı the  overall architecture ¦ before writing it.  Therefore, 
don't go into Too much derail, because thi s will  hide the structure. Try to describe each scene in one line.  
For example, a description of four scenes in The  Godfather might look like this:  
■ Michael saves the Don from assassination ­  hospital.  
■ Michael accuses police captain McCluskey of  working for Sollozzo.  The Captain slugs him.  Michael 
suggests ć kill the Captain and Sollozzo.  
■ Clemenza shows Michael ő execute Sollozzo ¥  Captain.  
Notice that only the single essential action of each scene  is listed. ọ keep your de scription to one 
or two lines,  you will be able to list your scene weave in a few pages.  Next to ś description, list 
any structure step (such  as ¹, plan, or apparent defeat) đ accomplished  during that scene. 
Some scenes will have these structure  tags, but many will not.  
Ã Be prepared to change your scene  weave when you start writing individual 
scenes.  
When it comes to actually writing a scene, you may find  ¶ basic action occurring in that scene 
× what you  thought. You will only know that for sure when you get  "inside" ś and write it. So 
be flexible. What's  important at this point ¢ process is İ an overview  of what you think the single 
main action of each scene will  be. 
Be aware ¶ average Hol lywood movie has forty to  seventy scenes. A novel ordinarily has twice 
that number  and, depending on length and genre, possibly ạ many  more.  
Your story may have subplots, or subsections, that when  woven together create ł. If ể 
more than  one subplot or subsection, label each scene ¾ plotline and  subsection number. This will 
allow you to ı śs  of each subplot as a separate unit and make sure each  subplot builds 
properly.  
Once ể the complete scene weave before you,  see if you need ū the following 
changes:  
■ Reorder scenes. First, focus on getting the overall  sequence ¦  right. Then ı the 
juxtapositions between individual  scenes.  
■ Combine scenes. Writers often create a new  scene for no other reason than İ in a good line of  
ß. Whenever possible, combine scenes ơ  each one is packed, but make sure each 
scene  accomplishes essentially one action.  
■ Cut or add scenes.  Always trim fat. Remember, story pacing has to do not only  ³ length of a 
scene but also ³ choice of  scenes. Once ể trimmed ē fat, you may find  gaps in 
ś weave that require a whole new scene. If  so, add it ª list ¢ right spot.  
Ã Order śs by structure, not  chronology.  
Most writers choose the next scene according to which  action (scene) comes next in time. The result 
is a padded  story with many useless scenes. Instead, you ě  choose a scene by how it furthers the 
development ¡  hero. If it doesn't further that development or set it up in a  crucial way, cut ś.  
P a g e  | 158 This ¼ guarantees that every scene Í  is essential and ¢ right order. Typically, 
you end ű  a chronological scene sequence, but not always.  
Ã Pay sp ecial attention ª juxtaposition  of scenes.  
Especially in film and television, where the change of  scene or story line is instantaneous, the 
juxtaposition of two  scenes can be more important than what happens in any  individual scene. țse 
juxtaposi tions, you ě look  first ­ contrast of content. In what way, if at all, does the  next scene 
comment ¯ previous scene?  
Then ı the contrast of proportion and pacing. Does  the next scene or section have the right 
importance and  length com pared ª previous scene or section?  
A good rule of thumb is this: find the line and keep the  line. There are some scenes —such as 
subplot scenes —that only set up the  narrative drive. Go ahead and put them in. But ō  never get 
away Ç narrativ e line tor too long without  ì collapsing.  
You can create powerful juxtapositions in all kinds of  ways. One ¡ best, especially in film and 
television, is  the juxtaposition between sight and sound. In this  ¼, you split these two 
communication tracks to  create a third meaning.  
 
M  
(by Tbea von Harbou and Fritz Lang, 1931)  
A classic  example ư ¼ occurs ¢ great German film  M. In M, a child murderer buys a 
little girl a balloon. ț  next scene, a woman prepares d inner ¥n calls for her  child, Elsie. As she 
continues to cē little girl's name,  the visual track splits Ç sound track, ¥  ¤ sees 
an empty stairwell, a block of apartments,  Elsie's empty chair, and her plate and spoon ­ kitchen  
table, while the ever more desperate cries ¡ mother  calling "Elsie!" are heard. The visual line ends 
³ shot  of a balloon that catches in some electrical wires ¥n  floats away. This contrast 
between the sound line ¥  visual lin e produces Â most heartbreaking  moments ¢ 
history of film.  
Perhaps î common ¼ of juxtaposition in  scene weave © crosscut. ț crosscut, 
you jump back  and forth between two or more lines of action. This  ¼ has two  main effects:  
1. It creates suspense, especially when you cut back  and forth at an increasing pace, as when someone 
is rushing to save a victim in danger.  
2. It compares two lines of action, two pieces of content,  and makes them equal. This expands your 
thematic  pattern. Anytime you jump back and forth between two lines  of action, you go from a simple 
linear development ø  story (usually of ô character) to show a deeper  pattern present in 
ấ society.  
An example ¡ content crosscut i s a sequence in M œ £ goes back and forth 
between a group of  cops and a group of criminals. Each is trying to figure out  ő find the child 
murderer, so the crosscut shows the  ¤ how two types of people they normally consider  opposi tes 
are in many ways identical.  
 
The Godfather  
(novel by Mario Puzo, Ó Mario Puzo and  Francis Ford Coppola,  1972)  
