MUlTISTRAND PLOT  SCENE WEAVE  
 
The television multistrand plot crosscuts between three  to five major story lines, each with its own 
hero. Telling this  many stories in about forty -five minutes (sixty minutes  minus commercials) means that 
no plotline can have much  depth in a ny one episode. The writers hope ū up for  that ê 
Đ ấ season ¥ many  seasons the show remains ¯ air.  
Ã In a multistrand weave, the quality ¡  overall story comes primarily Ç 
juxtaposition ¡  plotlines. You compare what ä people in a  minisociety are facing at 
Î time. The ¤  gets ẩ in compressed form how lead characters use  different 
solutions when trying to solve generally Î  problem.  
Ã With three to f ive plots, ō't cover  the twenty -two steps for any one line, but 
each must cover  the seven major structure steps. Anything less than the  seven steps means that 
that line isn't a complete story,  and ® will find it unnecessary and annoying.  
Ã With multiple main characters and so  many lines, you give shape ª overall 
story and  maintain narrative drive by making § of one line the  ¬ of another. This 
keeps £ from exploding  ever outward with, for example, five heroes, five  ¬s, 
myriad minor characters, and so on.  
One ¡ reasons ER and other TV dramas use this  multistrand crosscut ā it gives the episode 
dramatic  density. There is no lull ¢se stories. The ¤ sees  only the dramatic punch scenes of 
each plotline. ț case  of ER, creator Michael Crichton, the greatest ï  writer in Hollywood, 
figured out ő combine the benefits  ¡ medical drama ¥ action genre in one show. To  this 
mix, Crichton added  a character web that covers a broad range of classes,  races, ethnic backgrounds, 
nationalities, and genders.  That's a very potent and popular combination.  
 
